  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 1,
   "text": "Extras: the tail formula, moments, Chebyshev and Markov",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "d6c8049c-53f3-5296-9ab1-ed491327e774"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**The tail formula.** If $X$ can’t be negative ($X\\ge0$), you can get the mean straight from the CDF, without finding the PDF first:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "33acae4b-593e-5aba-ba17-841b87f3626d"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "E[X]=\\int_0^{\\infty}\\big(1-F_X(t)\\big)\\,dt\\qquad\\qquad E[N]=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}P(N>k)\\ \\ (N=0,1,2,\\dots)",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "f017b9e2-c705-4ab7-a4d1-e289ed6c90bf",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "5d0c04fd-0343-4ac4-b647-98627bcad82c"
    }
   ],
   "id": "e78bccb7-e5c2-5513-b8c0-4bf1e5483241"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "$1-F_X(t)=P(X>t)$ is the chance $X$ is still bigger than $t$. Adding those chances up gives the mean.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "2c3b5656-498c-516c-8236-c71817768aa7"
  },
  {
   "type": "worked_example",
   "title": "Example 11 · Tail formula · smooth and whole-number",
   "prompt": "(a) $F_X(x)=x^2$ on $[0,1]$. Find $E[X]$ with the tail formula. (b) $N$ is $1$, $2$ or $3$, each with chance $\\tfrac13$. Find $E[N]$ the same way.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "(a) **Spot:** $X\\ge0$ and a CDF is given. $1-F_X=1-t^2$ up to $t=1$, then $0$.",
     "latex": "E[X]=\\int_0^1(1-t^2)\\,dt=1-\\frac13=\\frac23"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "Same answer as the slope method in Topic 1.",
     "latex": ""
    },
    {
     "explanation": "(b) List $P(N>k)$ until it hits $0$.",
     "latex": "\\begin{gathered}P(N>0)=1,\\quad P(N>1)=\\frac23 \\\\ P(N>2)=\\frac13,\\quad P(N>3)=0\\end{gathered}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "Add them.",
     "latex": "E[N]=1+\\frac23+\\frac13=2"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "(a) $\\tfrac23$ (b) $2$",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "866c72ad-3e6b-5c5b-bb08-153e9f062e1d"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Moments** (Lecture 13). The $n$th **moment** is $E[X^n]$: $E[X]$ is the 1st, $E[X^2]$ the 2nd. A **central** moment measures from the mean instead. The variance is the 2nd central moment:",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "52e9fc11-81ed-4bab-a495-7e4b29f79a8b",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "061ca83b-a397-4cdf-b0d0-e3f23afbc005"
    }
   ],
   "id": "e5709ac9-9c14-5c73-a7ae-57e7d25039c5"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E\\big[(X-\\mu_X)^2\\big],\\qquad \\mu_X=E[X]",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "3221f942-5466-5266-bfef-a9164c68df98"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Chebyshev and Markov** (Practice 03, not in the lectures). They give an upper limit on a chance when you only know $E$ and Var. $\\varepsilon$ = how far from the mean; $a$ = a cutoff:",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "f017b9e2-c705-4ab7-a4d1-e289ed6c90bf",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "d7d6bffe-de13-4e08-924d-b1ebc73b82b9"
    }
   ],
   "id": "37f1701b-b11d-5d52-b420-2f4f39201651"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "\\text{Chebyshev: }\\ P\\big(|Y-\\mu_Y|\\ge\\varepsilon\\big)\\le\\frac{\\operatorname{Var}(Y)}{\\varepsilon^2}",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "e7a17ca2-92cf-576b-a27b-d8adc8762860"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "\\text{Markov }(Y\\ge0)\\text{: }\\ P(Y\\ge a)\\le\\frac{E[Y]}{a}",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "35640404-1167-5a2f-a26b-5d737f487473"
  },
  {
   "type": "worked_example",
   "title": "Example 12 · Chebyshev and Markov · plug in",
   "prompt": "(a) $\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=1$. How likely is $Y$ to land $2$ or more from its mean, at most? (b) $Y\\ge0$ with $E[Y]=3$. Bound $P(Y\\ge10)$.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "(a) **Chebyshev:** read off $\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=1$, $\\varepsilon=2$.",
     "latex": "P\\big(|Y-\\mu|\\ge2\\big)\\le\\frac{1}{2^2}=\\frac14"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "(b) **Markov:** read off $E[Y]=3$, $a=10$.",
     "latex": "P(Y\\ge10)\\le\\frac{3}{10}=0.3"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "(a) at most $\\tfrac14$ (b) at most $0.3$",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "b36238e1-a724-5b81-9628-82b7079fdcdd"
  },
